B.ce(1, [(56, 22, 30)],
     'Friend: Hi Sai! Where were you last week?\n'
     'Me: Hello Ravi! I went on a trip to Coorg with my family. It was amazing!\n'
     'Friend: Coorg? Where is it?\n'
     'Me: It is in Karnataka, midway between Mysuru and Mangaluru. It is full of rainforests, coffee estates and hills.\n'
     'Friend: What places did you see?\n'
     'Me: We climbed the Brahmagiri Hills and saw a panoramic view of the whole district. We crossed a rope bridge to Nisargadhama island and visited the Tibetan settlement at Bylakuppe, where Buddhist monks live.\n'
     'Friend: Did you see any animals?\n'
     'Me: Yes! Mahaseer fish in the Kaveri, langurs, macaques and Malabar squirrels in the trees, and elephants being bathed by their mahouts.\n'
     'Friend: Wow! What else did you do?\n'
     'Me: The best part was the adventure sports – river rafting, canoeing, rock rappelling and trekking. My heart was beating fast while rafting!\n'
     'Friend: You are lucky. I must go there too.\n'
     'Me: Sure. Go between September and March – the weather is perfect then. And don’t forget to taste Coorg coffee!')
B.ce(1, [(56, 31, 34), (57, 1, 2)],
     'Tourist: Excuse me, are you from Coorg? Could you tell me about this place?\n'
     'Me: Yes, I am a Kodava. Welcome to Coorg! How can I help you?\n'
     'Tourist: What are the best places to visit nearby?\n'
     'Me: You must see the Brahmagiri Hills, Nisargadhama island, the coffee plantations and the Tibetan monastery at Bylakuppe. If you like adventure, try river rafting in the Kaveri.\n'
     'Tourist: I have heard that the people of Coorg are different. Is it true?\n'
     'Me: Yes. We Kodavus are a proud martial race. One story says that a part of Alexander’s Greek army settled here; that is why we wear a long black coat called “kuppia” with an embroidered waist-belt. Another theory says we are of Arab origin, as the kuppia looks like the Arab “kuffia”.\n'
     'Tourist: Interesting! What else is special?\n'
     'Me: We are known for our hospitality and our tales of valour. The Coorg Regiment is one of the most decorated in the Indian Army, and General Cariappa, India’s first Commander-in-Chief, was a Coorgi. We are also the only people in India allowed to carry firearms without a licence.\n'
     'Tourist: Thank you so much. I already feel at home!\n'
     'Me: You are welcome. Enjoy our coffee and our hospitality!')
B.ce(1, [(57, 3, 9)],
     'Saturday, 10th January, 2027                                                   Coorg, 9:30 p.m.\n'
     'Dear Diary,\n'
     'Today was one of the most beautiful days of my life. Coorg is truly the “Scotland of India”.\n'
     'Early in the morning we climbed the Brahmagiri Hills. The cool breeze and the panoramic view of the misty valleys mesmerized me. '
     'Then we walked across a rope bridge to the island of Nisargadhama, where we saw deer and elephants being bathed in the Kaveri by their mahouts. '
     'In the afternoon we walked through coffee plantations under the tall trees of the rainforest; the smell of coffee was everywhere. '
     'I saw langurs, macaques and a Malabar squirrel watching us from the canopy, and kingfishers diving for Mahaseer fish.\n'
     'The most thrilling part was river rafting. I was scared at first, but then I enjoyed it a lot.\n'
     'In the evening we visited Bylakuppe and saw Buddhist monks in red, ochre and yellow robes. Our host told us stories about the brave Kodavus and their Greek or Arab origin.\n'
     'I am tired but very happy. I will never forget Coorg.')
B.ce(1, [(57, 10, 14)],
     'Saturday, 25th July                                                Dhekiabari Tea Estate, 9:00 p.m.\n'
     'Dear Diary,\n'
     'Today I visited Pranjol’s home at the Dhekiabari Tea Estate in Assam. It was a dream come true!\n'
     'From the train I saw a sea of tea bushes stretching as far as the eye could see. Assam has the largest concentration of tea plantations in the world. '
     'I saw women with bamboo baskets on their backs plucking the tender leaves – it looked like a picture.\n'
     'Pranjol’s father, Mr Barua, the manager, was surprised when I told him the legends of tea – the Chinese emperor whose boiling water got a delicious flavour from fallen leaves, and Bodhidharma, from whose eyelids ten tea plants grew. '
     'I also told him that tea was first drunk in China as early as 2700 B.C., and that it came to Europe only in the sixteenth century.\n'
     'He explained that it was the second flush, the sprouting period from May to July, which yields the best tea. The first flush is from March to May.\n'
     'I was in awe of the beauty of the estate and the hard work behind every cup of tea. I am going to read more about it.\nRajvir', lid='en5')

