B.ce(2, (58, 18, 24),
     'Rajvir: Good evening, sir. Your tea estate is beautiful. May I ask you a few questions about tea?\n'
     'Mr Barua: Of course, Rajvir. I am happy to see your interest.\n'
     'Rajvir: Sir, what are tea clones?\n'
     'Mr Barua: Clones are tea plants grown from cuttings of the best bushes. They give a uniform, high-quality crop. We develop new clones to get better yield and flavour.\n'
     'Rajvir: I have read about the first flush and the second flush. What is the difference?\n'
     'Mr Barua: The first flush is the first sprouting period, from about March to May. The second flush, from May to July, gives the best tea with a strong flavour.\n'
     'Rajvir: What kind of weather does tea need?\n'
     'Mr Barua: Tea grows best in a cool, moist climate with plenty of rain – about 150 to 250 cm a year – and well-drained, sloping land. Assam is ideal.\n'
     'Rajvir: Is it true that tea was first drunk in China?\n'
     'Mr Barua: Yes. It was drunk in China as far back as 2700 B.C. In India, the British started tea gardens in Assam in the nineteenth century.\n'
     'Rajvir: Thank you, sir. I have learnt so much today.\n'
     'Mr Barua: You are a curious young man. Keep learning!')
B.ce(2, (58, 25, 29),
     'Saturday, 25th July                                                Dhekiabari Tea Estate, 10:00 p.m.\n'
     'Dear Diary,\n'
     'Today my son Pranjol came home for the holidays with his classmate Rajvir. I took both the boys around the tea garden in my jeep.\n'
     'I expected the city boy to be bored, but Rajvir surprised me. He knew so much about tea! He told me the Chinese legend of the emperor and the boiling water, and the Indian legend of Bodhidharma. '
     'He even knew that tea was drunk in China as early as 2700 B.C. and reached Europe only in the sixteenth century, first as medicine.\n'
     'When I told him that we were in the middle of the second sprouting period, he immediately said, “Yes, May to July – the second flush yields the best tea.” I was really impressed.\n'
     'He watched the women plucking the leaves with great interest and asked about tea clones and the weather the plants need.\n'
     'Pranjol, who has grown up on the estate, hardly shows such interest! Rajvir is a bright, well-read and polite boy. I am happy that my son has such a good friend. I hope he visits us again.\n'
     'Mr Barua')
